<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jamestown.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-10">10 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">James I charters the Virginia Company</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">King James I granted a charter to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> Company's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and Plymouth branches to settle the North American coast. The London company founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> the following year.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-12">12 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">First Union Flag is adopted for English and Scottish ships</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A royal proclamation of James I ordered ships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> to fly a new flag combining the crosses of St George and St Andrew. St Patrick's saltire was added in 1801.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jamestown.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-20">20 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Virginia Company ships sail for America</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Susan Constant, Godspeed and Discovery left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> with about 105 colonists. In May 1607 they founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a>, the first permanent English settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-26">26 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Shakespeare's King Lear is performed before King James I</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-shakespeare">William Shakespeare</a>'s tragedy King Lear, based on the legend of Leir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, was performed before King James I, the earliest performance on record, noted in the Stationers' Register. The play was probably written in late 1605 or early 1606.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI and I</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-zsitvatorok" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peace-of-zsitvatorok.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty · Peace</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-zsitvatorok">Peace of Zsitvatorok</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Peace of Zsitvatorok was signed on 11 November 1606 at the mouth of the Zsitva River in Hungary, ending the Long Turkish War between the Habsburg Monarchy and the Ottoman Empire.</p></div></li></ol></section>
